<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

People share their screens in meetings, record tutorials, stream games, and show support staff what went
wrong. When they do, the page they share shows everything it shows them: the balance of their bank account,
the preview of a private message, a notification badge, an API key. Native apps on some platforms can hide
such things while the screen is recorded. Web pages can't.

This specification lets a page do the same:

<div class=example id=example-intro>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (screen-capture: active) {
      .balance, .message-preview, .api-key {
        color: transparent;
        text-shadow: 0 0 0.6em rgb(0 0 0 / 0.6);
      }
      .notification-count { background-color: transparent; color: transparent; }
    }
    </pre>
</div>

Telling a page that the screen is being captured would also tell it that the user is in a meeting, or
streaming, or talking to support, and would let a page look different to the people watching than it does
when nobody is. So the feature is a <em>protected</em> media feature [[!MEDIA-FEATURE-PRIVACY]]: rules that
depend on it can change only colors, shadows, outlines, and decorations, and nothing the page can measure
changes. Script that asks gets ''screen-capture/none''.

<div class=example id=example-overlay>
    Hiding images works with an overlay that is always there and is transparent until a capture starts:

    <pre highlight=css>
    .avatar { position: relative; }
    .avatar::after {
      content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 0; pointer-events: none;
      background-color: transparent;
    }
    @media (screen-capture: active) {
      .avatar::after { background-color: #9ca3af; }
    }
    </pre>
</div>


<h2 id=feature>The '@media/screen-capture' feature</h2>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: screen-capture
Value: none | active
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The '@media/screen-capture' media feature is a [=protected media feature=] whose [=observable value=] is
''screen-capture/none''. Every document uses [=protected evaluation=] for it.

Its [=actual value=] in a [=document=] |d| is:

: <dfn value for=@media/screen-capture>active</dfn>
:: |d| is [=being captured=].
: <dfn value for=@media/screen-capture>none</dfn>
:: Otherwise. This value evaluates to false in a [=boolean context=].

<div class=note>
    Because of protected evaluation, a rule under ''screen-capture/active'' can only change the
    [=paint-only properties=], such as 'color', 'background-color', 'box-shadow', and 'outline'. Its other
    declarations, such as <code>display: none</code>, have no effect. <code>matchMedia("(screen-capture:
    active)")</code> never matches, and no <code>change</code> event is ever fired for it.
</div>

<h3 id=capture-definition>Being captured</h3>

A document |d| is <dfn export>being captured</dfn> when |d|'s [=Document/visibility state=] is
"<code>visible</code>", and the user agent knows that the part of the screen where |d| is displayed is
included in one of these:

1. A capture that the user agent makes, of a tab, window, or screen, for
    {{MediaDevices/getDisplayMedia()}} or for its own casting or recording features.
1. A screen recording or screen sharing session that the operating system reports to the user agent.
1. A mirror of the display on another display, if the operating system reports it.

These are not captures, and must not make any document [=being captured|be captured=]:

* Assistive technology, such as screen readers, screen magnifiers, and tools that read text from the screen
    or caption it.
* The user agent's own use of page images, such as tab thumbnails and previews.
* A single screenshot.

The user agent should let the user exclude particular capture sessions, or particular apps that capture the
screen, and should not count a remote desktop session in which the remote display is the only place the user
sees the document.

<div class=note>
    Platforms report different things. Android 15 tells apps when their windows appear in a screen
    recording [[ANDROID-SCREEN-RECORDING]]. iOS and iPadOS report when the screen is being recorded,
    mirrored, or sent to another display. Desktop operating systems don't generally tell apps that another
    app is capturing the screen, so on desktop computers the feature mostly reflects captures made by the
    user agent, such as sharing a tab or window into a video meeting in the same browser.
</div>

<h3 id=timing>Timing</h3>

When a capture made by the user agent starts, the user agent must not include a frame showing a document in
the capture until it has painted that document with its new [=painted style=]. When the operating system reports a capture
that has already started, the user agent must paint the new painted style as soon as it can.

When a capture ends, the user agent should paint the new painted style at the next rendering opportunity.

<h3 id=same-picture>The same picture for everyone</h3>

The user agent must show the user the same painted content that it includes in a capture. It must not paint a
document one way for the user's screen and another way for a capture.

<div class=note>
    This rule is what stops a page from showing the people watching something different from what the user
    sees. See [[#security-evasion]].
</div>

<h3 id=user-control>User control</h3>

The feature is [=under user control=]. When the user turns it off, the [=actual value=] is
''screen-capture/none'' in every document. The user agent must also let the user turn the feature off for a
single capture session, while it is running, for example with a control in its sharing indicator.

The user agent should tell the user, while a capture is running, when a document in the capture is painted
differently because of this feature, for example with a message in its sharing indicator such as "Some
content on this page is hidden while you share".

<div class=note>
    User agents know when this happens: it is when a document's painted style differs from its
    [=observable style=] because of this feature. Telling the user doesn't tell the page anything.
</div>


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

<h3 id=privacy-unprotected>What a capture reveals</h3>

If a page could find out that the user's screen is being captured, it would learn:

* **What the user is doing.** They are in a meeting, presenting, streaming, recording a tutorial, or
    getting help.
* **Who else can see.** Someone else is watching the screen, now or later.
* **When.** The moments a capture starts and stops are the same in every document on the screen, so a
    tracker present in two of them could link them.

Pages could use that to target streamers, to change what they show when a support agent is watching, or to
record when the user is in meetings.

<h3 id=privacy-protected>Why the feature is always protected</h3>

Every use of the feature is to hide something visual, and hiding something visual doesn't need the page to know.
The feature is therefore always evaluated with [=protected evaluation=], with no way for a page to ask for
more:

<table class=data>
    <thead><tr><th>Channel<th>What a page sees during a capture
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Script
            <td><code>matchMedia()</code> reports ''screen-capture/none''; computed styles are unchanged
        <tr><td>Layout
            <td>No box changes size or position, because only paint-only properties change
        <tr><td>Network
            <td>No resource is fetched or not fetched because of the capture
        <tr><td>Timing
            <td>No events are fired, no transitions run, and no performance entries are created
        <tr><td>Embedded content
            <td>A frame can hide its own content, and learns no more than the top-level document does, which
                is nothing
</table>

Because nothing is revealed, the feature needs no delay, no restriction in frames, and no special behavior in
private browsing modes, and it can take effect before the first frame of the capture.

<h3 id=privacy-fingerprinting>Fingerprinting and linking visits</h3>

The feature adds no information to a fingerprint and no moment that could link visits, because no document can
observe its value.

<h3 id=privacy-residual>Remaining risks</h3>

* **Interaction.** A page could paint a message such as "click here" only during a capture, and learn that a
    capture is happening if someone clicks. This needs the user, or a viewer, to act on it.
* **The page's own capture.** If the user lets a page capture its own tab, the page sees the painted result.
    It already knows a capture is happening, because it started it.
* **Viewers.** The people watching can see that some content is hidden. That tells them the page hides
    content, not what it is.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

<h3 id=security-not-protection>Not a way to protect content</h3>

The feature reduces accidental exposure. It isn't a security boundary, and it isn't a way to protect content
from copying:

* The user agent only knows about some captures. Most desktop operating systems don't report captures by other
    apps.
* Screenshots aren't reported.
* A camera pointed at the screen isn't reported.
* The user can turn the feature off.

Pages must not rely on the feature to keep anything secret.

<h3 id=security-evasion>Looking different to the people watching</h3>

A page could hide content during captures to avoid being seen by the people who would catch it: a fraudulent
payment request hidden from a family member watching over a screen share, a scam hidden from a bank's fraud
team, or abusive content hidden from a victim trying to record it.

[[#same-picture]] means the page can't show the user one thing and the viewers another. What the viewers see is
what the user sees. A page can still change what both see while a capture is running, and the user could
record the page without the hidden content. To prevent that from being used to hide evidence:

* The user can turn the feature off for a capture session while it runs, and the capture then shows the page
    as it looks without a capture.
* The user agent tells the user when content is hidden, so the user and the viewers know that what they see
    isn't the whole page.

<h3 id=security-screenshots>Screenshots</h3>

Screenshots are excluded because a page can't hide anything before a screenshot is taken. Reporting them would
only let pages detect screenshots, which could be used to punish users for keeping records.

<h3 id=security-other-captures>Captures started by other pages</h3>

A page that asks the user to share another tab can make that tab hide its content, but only in the capture the
page itself receives. It learns nothing about the other tab it wouldn't see in the capture anyway.


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

<h3 id=alt-unprotected>An ordinary media feature</h3>

An ordinary media feature would let pages use any CSS, and script, during captures, such as switching to a
larger layout for presenting. It was not chosen because it would reveal what [[#privacy-unprotected]] describes,
and every case it adds beyond hiding content changes what the page shows the viewers rather than protecting
the user.

<h3 id=alt-capture-only>Hiding content in the capture only</h3>

A property such as <code>capture-visibility: hidden</code> could hide an element in captures while the user still
sees it on their screen, which is more convenient for the user. It was not chosen for two reasons. It would let
a page show the viewers something different from what the user sees, which is what makes fraud during screen
sharing work. And it could only apply to captures the user agent makes, because an operating system captures
the pixels on the screen. Some operating systems can exclude a whole window from captures, but not part of one.

<h3 id=alt-event>A capture event for script</h3>

Android 15 tells apps when their windows are recorded. A similar event for script would have the same problems
as an ordinary media feature, and pages don't need it to hide content.

<h3 id=alt-screenshots>Including screenshots</h3>

See [[#security-screenshots]].


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<p class=issue>Should a display mirrored to a projector count? Presenters would benefit, and the operating system
usually knows, but mirroring isn't always to an audience.

<p class=issue>'opacity' and 'filter: blur()' are the most natural ways to hide content, and they aren't
paint-only properties. See the open issue in [[MEDIA-FEATURE-PRIVACY]].

<p class=issue>Should operating systems be asked to report captures to apps, as Android 15 does, so that the
feature works for desktop video meeting apps?


<h2 id=questionnaire class=no-num>Appendix: Security and privacy questionnaire</h2>

*This section is non-normative.* These are the answers to the questions in
[[SECURITY-PRIVACY-QUESTIONNAIRE]].

: What information does this feature expose, and for what purposes?
:: None to the page. It lets the page's appearance change during a screen capture, so the page can hide
    personal details.
: Do features in your specification expose the minimum amount of information necessary?
:: Yes. The page can't observe the value.
: Do the features expose personal information, PII, or information derived from either?
:: No.
: How do the features deal with sensitive information?
:: They help pages hide it during captures, without revealing that a capture is happening.
: Does data exposed carry related but distinct information that may not be obvious to users?
:: No data is exposed. See [[#privacy-residual]] for what a page can learn with the user's help.
: Do the features introduce state that persists across browsing sessions?
:: No.
: Do the features expose information about the underlying platform to origins?
:: No.
: Does this specification allow an origin to send data to the underlying platform?
:: No.
: Do features enable access to device sensors?
:: No.
: Do features enable new script execution or loading mechanisms?
:: No. Rules under the feature can't load resources.
: Do features allow an origin to access other devices?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin some measure of control over a user agent's native UI?
:: No. The user agent's sharing indicator may say that content is hidden; the page doesn't control it.
: What temporary identifiers do the features create or expose to the web?
:: None.
: How does this specification distinguish between behavior in first-party and third-party contexts?
:: It doesn't need to, because no context can observe the value.
: How do the features work in the context of a browser's Private Browsing or Incognito mode?
:: The same as in normal browsing.
: Does this specification have both "Security Considerations" and "Privacy Considerations" sections?
:: Yes.
: Do features enable origins to downgrade default security protections?
:: No.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature is kept alive in BFCache?
:: It isn't visible, so it isn't being captured. When it is restored, it is painted for the current state.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature gets disconnected?
:: Nothing is kept.
: Does your spec define when and how new kinds of errors should be raised?
:: No.
: Does your feature allow sites to learn about the user's use of assistive technology?
:: No. Assistive technology doesn't count as a capture.
: What should this questionnaire have asked?
:: Whether a feature can make a page look different to people other than the user, and whether the user can
    tell.
